          %h2#a_new_formulation_for_classical_index_theory A new formulation for classical index theory
          %p
            Classical real or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+geometry"} complex analytic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theory"} index theory
            may be described as a setting that contains:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Roch+theorem"} Riemann-Roch
                type theorems, such as Bismut’s Riemann-Roch theorem for a general smooth proper morphism of complex analytic spaces, Grothendieck’s Riemann-Roch theorem for a morphism of schemes, or Riou’s motivic Riemann-Roch theorem for a projective morphism of schemes, and
            %li
              %p
                Index theorems for elliptic operators or elliptic complexes on real analytic manifolds (that actually also give Riemann-Roch results, when the elliptic complex is induced by the
                = succeed "-operator" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mo ∂
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{\partial}
            %li
              %p
                Equivariant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah-Singer+index+theorems"} Atiyah-Singer index theorems
                and noncommutative Connes-Moscovisci local index theorems.
          %p
            One may formulate many of these results in an essentially unified setting by using index theory on derived analytic stacks over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
          %p Recall also that one may solve a linear partial differential equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(\partial)=0
          %p
            when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            has constant coefficients by using the analytic continuation of the complex-valued function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,s)\mapsto P(x)^s
          %h2#the_challenge_of_global_analytic_index_theory The challenge of global analytic index theory
          %p
            Global analytic index theory should give a setting for index theory on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+spaces"} global analytic spaces
            that gives back usual index theorems when one works over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            equipped with its archimedean norm. It should also give back the p-adic index theorems of Christol-Mebkhout when one works in a Frobenius-equivariant setting over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_p
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℚ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_p
          %p
            The starting point of this approach should be given by the settlement of a global analytic derived version of Kashiwara and Schapira’s microlocal formulation of various index theorems. It is essential, for this approach to work, to have a viewpoint on the microlocalization techniques that is very close to Toen and Vezzosi’s loop space approach to the Chern character, through Hochschild and negative cyclic homology of rigid symmetric monoidal stacks of
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-stacks," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+duality"} Grothendieck duality
            adapted to global analytic derived geometry, because it is necessary to define microlocal Hochschild homology in Kashiwara and Schapira’s approach to index theory.
          %h2#an_important_difficulty An important difficulty
          %p
            A difficulty to be overcome in the seek for a global analytic index theory is that the theory of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-coefficients" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of characteristic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            (Ogus’ article on the infinitesimal site). There is also not yet a clear idea of what the analog of
            = succeed "-constructible" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p
            For example, if one mimicks the approach of Kashiwara and Schapira in the characteristic
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            setting, one has to replace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}/\mathbb{R}
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi 𝔽
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝔽
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{\mathbb{F}}_p/\mathbb{F}_p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathcal{O}_X,\mathbb{C}_M)
            associated to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M/\mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔽
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M/\mathbb{F}_p
            a projective smooth variety, has de Rham cohomology an
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi 𝔽
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar{\mathbb{F}}_p
          %p
            Two approaches to
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            were developed by Berthelot and Mebkhout-Narvaez-Macarro. One may try to extend this last approach (using weak formal schemes over
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_p
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            Another (maybe more promising) approach is to get inspiration from Buium’s differential calculus over the integers and Madsen/Hesselholt’s results on topological cyclic homology: making a topological version of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+global+analytic+geometry"} spectral global analytic geometry
          %h2#pursuing_global_analytic_trace_kernels Pursuing global analytic trace kernels
          %p
            Recall that a trace kernel in Kashiwara and Schapira’s sense is a
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %mo ×
                        %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_{M\times M}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            together with a sequence of morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_\Delta\to K\to \omega_\Delta
          %p
            and the corresponding class is a section of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi k
                      %mi Δ
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu hom(k_\Delta,\omega_\Delta)
          %p
            In the coherent setting, the analog of a trace kernel associated to a coherent
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒪
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            is given by the sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi D
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi hol
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{O}_\Delta\to \mathcal{F}\boxtimes D\mathcal{F}\to \omega^{hol}_\Delta.
          %p
            In the
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}
            is given by the sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo ⊠
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi Δ
                    %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{B}_\Delta\to \mathcal{M}\boxtimes D_\mathcal{D}\mathcal{M}\to \mathcal{B}^\vee_\Delta
          %p
            and one sends it to the trace kernel given by taking the de Rham functor (tensor product operation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_{X\times X}\otimes_\mathcal{D}-
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mi E
                  %mo ⊠
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi ℰ
                  %msup
                    %mi ℳ
                    %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi Δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo ∨
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{B}_\Delta^E\to \mathcal{M}^E\boxtimes D_\mathcal{E}\mathcal{M}^E\to \mathcal{B}^{E\vee}_\Delta
          %p because one has a canonical isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %msub
                    %mi Hom
                    %mi ℰ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi E
                  %mo ,
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ℬ
                    %mi Δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo ∨
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ∼
                  %mi μ
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}Hom_{\mathcal{E}}(\mathcal{B}_\Delta^E,\mathcal{B}_\Delta^{E\vee})
                  \\overset{\sim}{\longrightarrow}
                  \\mu hom(\mathbb{C}_\Delta,\omega_\Delta).
          %p
            The problem is now the following: given an
            = succeed "-constructible" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi DF
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}_\Delta\to F\boxtimes DF\to \omega_\Delta.
          %p
            How can one formulate this construction in terms of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            The easiest thing to try is to simply use the (awful) sheaf of rings
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}^\infty
            of infinite order microdifferential operators. Indeed, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is an
            = succeed "-constructible" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu Hom(F,\mathcal{O})
            is naturally an
            = succeed "-module." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}^\infty
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
            = succeed "-module," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℰ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}
            = succeed "-module." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}^\infty
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℰ
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}^\infty
            and to find a nice class of
            = succeed "-modules," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℰ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{E}^\infty
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
          %p
            A first step in this global direction is to develop an index theory that works in a uniform way over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ℚ
                    %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_p
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} DR
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝒪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{DR}(\mathcal{O})
            = succeed "-modules," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒟
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}^\infty
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            One should not try to develop a theory of
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            Once the theory of
            = succeed "-adic" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %h2#a_possible_set_of_constraints_for_a_coherent_theory A possible set of constraints for a coherent theory
          %p One should be able to define global analytic analogs of:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The derived duality functor on coherent
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒟
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒪
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
                = succeed "-topology," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %li
              %p The Grothendieck six operations on:
          %ul
            %li
              coherent
              = succeed "-modules," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %li
              coherent
              = succeed "-modules," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒪
                        %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}^t
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}^t
              (definition due to P. Schapira) is the sheaf for the
              = succeed "-topology" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              with Berkovich interior
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi R
                      %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overset{\circ}{R}
              by the subsheaf of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mover
                        %mi R
                        %mo ∘
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}_{\overset{\circ}{R}}
              given by functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              with temperate growth, that one may define as the
              = succeed "-module" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝒪
                      %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}^b
              of bounded holomorphic functions.
            %li
              sub-analytic torsion étale (i.e., Artin-Verdier) or more generally motivic analytic sheaves (using the sheaf-cosheaf formalism of Lurie’s book higher algebra, generalized to the
              = succeed "-localic" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %li
              global analytic holonomic
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              = succeed "-module" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              = succeed "-tensor" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overset{*}{\otimes}
            %li
              One may also define the Grothendieck six operations on general
              = succeed "-modules." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒟
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}^\infty
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The Grothendieck six operations on sub-analytic Hodge structures (given by a loop space construction of the Hodge filtration, without denominators, together with a Betti structure, given by topological
                = succeed "-theory" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                = succeed "-structure" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                base).
            %li
              %p
                The Chern character in various situations, using either the formalism of Toen-Vezzosi or the formalism of Kashiwara-Schapira, applied to the following stacks of rigid (maybe non) symmetric monoidal
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
                = succeed "-topos" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              the stack
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi Perf
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\mapsto Perf(X)
              of perfect
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒪
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            %li
              the stack
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ↦
                    %msub
                      %mi Const
                      %mi proet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\mapsto Const_{proet}(X,\mathcal{C})
              of constructible pro-etale sub-analytic sheaves with values in a symmetric monoidal stable presentable
              = succeed "-category," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℚ
                        %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}_p
            %li
              one would also like to treat the case of (at least holonomic)
              = succeed "-modules," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo !
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\otimes^!,\otimes^*)
              on
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
          %ol
            %li
              The treatment of
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒟
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi 𝒟
                        %mi q
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}_q
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-commutative+global+analytic+index+theory"} non-commutative global analytic index theory
          %h2#derived_index_theory Derived index theory
          %p
            Using the methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+microlocalization"} derived microlocalization
            and Kashiwara and Schapira’s
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+formulation+of+index+theory"} microlocal formulation of index theory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masaki+Kashiwara"} Masaki Kashiwara
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Schapira"} Pierre Schapira
            %em Microlocal Euler classes and Hochschild homology
            %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1203.4869"} arXiv
          %p One may be very tempted to use the following reference, to get a better way of looking at Deligne (or global analytic motivic) cohomology, in use for the Grothendieck-Riemann-Roch theorem:
          %ul
            %li
              J. P. Pridham,
              %em A K-theoretic interpretation of real Deligne cohomology
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/pdf/1411.5705v1.pdf"} arXiv
